WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.900
Why can the RNA copied from a gene differ from the message a ribosome reads?

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.880
Watch one new transcript grow.

3
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.080
Some parts will stay, some will leave, and two new end marks will appear.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.660
These planned changes are RNA processing.

5
00:00:20.317 --> 00:00:25.077
The first copy is a primary transcript, also called pre-mRNA.

6
00:00:26.457 --> 00:00:29.357
Blue exons are retained in the processed RNA.

7
00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.457
Red introns lie between them and will be removed.

8
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.557
Each decision changes this same moving transcript.

9
00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:41.737
Processing can begin before transcription ends.

10
00:00:43.017 --> 00:00:48.417
A methylated guanosine cap attaches to the 5′ end through an unusual triphosphate

11
00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:54.057
bridge. That bridge leaves no ordinary exposed 5′ end for an exonuclease.

12
00:00:55.317 --> 00:00:58.717
The cap also recruits proteins for export and translation.

13
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.160
Near the other end, an RNA signal guides a cut.

14
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.940
Poly(A) polymerase adds adenosines without copying DNA.

15
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.960
The tail is added to RNA, rather than copied from the gene.

16
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.540
Its binding proteins support stability and translation.

17
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.630
Now zoom into one intron.

18
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.870
In the major human class, its ends usually carry GU and AG.

19
00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:31.210
A branch point inside contains the adenosine that starts the reaction.

20
00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.530
A pyrimidine-rich stretch lies before the final site.

21
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:39.330
Small nuclear RNAs help place these sites.

22
00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.910
U1 recognizes the 5′ splice site.

23
00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:47.690
U2 holds the branch region with its adenosine exposed.

24
00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.890
Other particles join and rearrange the assembly.

25
00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.970
Together, these RNAs and proteins form a spliceosome anew on each intron.

26
00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.970
The branch adenosine attacks the 5′ splice site.

27
00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:07.110
The upstream exon comes free, and the intron bends into a lariat.

28
00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.050
Look at the free edge glowing gold.

29
00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.290
Which exposed end must move next to join the exons?

30
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:24.563
The freed end of the upstream exon attacks the 3′ splice site.

31
00:02:25.823 --> 00:02:28.123
Both exons join, and the lariat leaves.

32
00:02:29.423 --> 00:02:32.443
Two attacks, two bond swaps, and the intron is gone.

33
00:02:33.533 --> 00:02:35.393
Pull back to the whole transcript.

34
00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:39.813
Introns leave as lariats, and exons meet in order.

35
00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:44.653
The transcript is shorter, but the retained exons are connected.

36
00:02:45.913 --> 00:02:48.233
The cap and tail remain at opposite ends.

37
00:02:49.453 --> 00:02:52.273
A processed message is now ready for later steps.

38
00:02:53.317 --> 00:02:55.337
Ends marked; segments chosen.

39
00:02:56.577 --> 00:03:00.417
That is how one primary transcript becomes a messenger RNA.

40
00:03:01.717 --> 00:03:04.637
The end marks also help protect and use the message.

41
00:03:05.837 --> 00:03:11.377
The rule applies to protein-coding RNA polymerase II transcripts in eukaryotic

42
00:03:11.377 --> 00:03:17.657
nuclei. Processing marks transcript ends and chooses which segments join into

43
00:03:17.657 --> 00:03:18.477
messenger RNA.
